{"timestamp":"2026-08-31T02:41:49.789Z","ordinal":8419,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_07a651664efba6ab016a94e9ed30b087d0b2cbdb836e202409","status":"completed","call_id":"call_onniyRbHgsTdzTT3e0e2ARcJ","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\n  cmd: \"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/serro/.codex/skills/pr-refetch-fix/SKILL.md && rg -n \\\"TIDY-442|PR #570|pr-refetch-fix|bounded refetch|reviewThreads\\\" /Users/serro/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md\",\n  workdir: \"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/nucleus\",\n  yield_time_ms: 10000,\n  max_output_tokens: 30000\n});\ntext(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a055b1-b0ee-7061-8828-4da29bf187dc","create_time":1788144104.987005}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-31T02:41:49.875Z","ordinal":8420,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a053e7-239f-7703-8a5b-d9d8ac22d6de","turn_id":"01a055b1-b0ee-7061-8828-4da29bf187dc","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-2f543004-0f10-4b69-930f-77c7b49ed031","process_id":"21815","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,260p' /Users/serro/.codex/skills/pr-refetch-fix/SKILL.md && rg -n \"TIDY-442|PR #570|pr-refetch-fix|bounded refetch|reviewThreads\" /Users/serro/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/nucleus","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/serro/.codex/skills/pr-refetch-fix/SKILL.md","name":"SKILL.md","path":"/Users/serro/.codex/skills/pr-refetch-fix/SKILL.md"},{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n 'TIDY-442|PR #570|pr-refetch-fix|bounded refetch|reviewThreads' /Users/serro/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md","query":"TIDY-442|PR #570|pr-refetch-fix|bounded refetch|reviewThreads","path":"MEMORY.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"---\nname: pr-refetch-fix\ndescription: Refetch the latest GitHub PR review feedback reliably before applying fixes. Use when a user asks to re-check review comments after a push, says there are newer PR comments that were missed, or when timestamp-only PR comment queries may be incomplete or misleading. This skill is for GitHub PR review triage where unresolved review threads, inline comments, review bodies, and issue comments must be checked together.\n---\n\n# PR Refetch Fix\n\nRefetch PR feedback from the right GitHub surfaces before deciding there are no new issues. Do not rely only on a timestamp cutoff against inline review comments.\n\n## Core Rule\n\nWhen the user asks for \"newer PR comments\", \"latest review issues\", or says comments were missed:\n\n1. Check current unresolved review threads first.\n2. Treat timestamp filtering as secondary, not primary.\n3. Verify every still-open thread against the current worktree before fixing or dismissing it.\n\nThe failure mode this skill prevents is:\n\n- querying only `pulls/{n}/comments` after an incorrect cutoff time\n- missing comments posted in review threads or review bodies\n- missing still-unresolved older threads that remain actionable after newer pushes\n\n## Workflow\n\n### 1. Identify the PRs and auth context\n\nUse the user's existing GitHub auth context if they specified one earlier, such as:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\n```\n\nPreserve that context for every `gh` call in the workflow.\n\n### 1a. Work only in the PR's corresponding worktree and branch\n\nBefore checking comments or making fixes:\n\n1. switch to the PR's dedicated worktree\n2. confirm the checked-out branch is the PR head branch\n3. inspect and edit only in that worktree\n\nDo not:\n\n- inspect comments against the main repo checkout if the PR has a separate worktree\n- validate a review comment against `next`, `dev`, or another sibling PR branch\n- make fixes in one worktree and assume they apply to another PR branch\n\nIf multiple PRs are involved, handle each PR in its own worktree independently.\n\nMinimum check:\n\n```bash\ngit branch --show-current\ngit status --short\ngit remote -v\n```\n\nUse the worktree that already backs the PR whenever available. Treat that worktree as the source of truth for:\n\n- whether a comment is already fixed\n- whether a thread is stale\n- what code should be edited\n\n### 2. Do not start with timestamp-only filtering\n\nDo not begin with:\n\n- only `gh pr view --json comments`\n- only `gh api repos/.../pulls/<n>/comments`\n- only \"comments after last pushed commit time\"\n\nThose can miss actionable review feedback.\n\n### 3. Fetch unresolved review threads with GraphQL\n\nThis is the primary source of truth for \"what still needs attention\".\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql -f query='\nquery {\n  repository(owner:\"OWNER\", name:\"REPO\") {\n    pullRequest(number:PR_NUMBER) {\n      reviewThreads(first:100) {\n        nodes {\n          isResolved\n          path\n          comments(first:20) {\n            nodes {\n              body\n              author { login }\n              createdAt\n              outdated\n              url\n              diffHunk\n              originalLine\n              line\n            }\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}'\n```\n\nIf there may be more than 100 threads, page through the connection instead of assuming the first page is complete.\n\n### 4. Also check the other review surfaces\n\nAfter review threads, inspect:\n\n1. PR reviews\n2. PR issue comments\n3. Inline review comments if needed for raw export or extra metadata\n\nUseful commands:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\n```\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\n```\n\nUse these to supplement threads, not replace them.\n\n### 5. Use timestamps carefully\n\nIf the user explicitly wants \"since the last push\", compute the cutoff from the exact pushed commit, not a guessed time.\n\nRules:\n\n- use the exact commit SHA that was pushed\n- use its absolute timestamp in UTC\n- if the user seems confused about \"today\", \"after that push\", or \"latest\", mention exact dates/times\n\nEven with an accurate cutoff, do not exclude unresolved review threads just because the thread started earlier. A still-unresolved thread can remain actionable after later pushes.\n\n### 6. Classify findings before editing\n\nFor each thread or comment:\n\n`Actionable`\n- thread is unresolved, and\n- the comment still matches the current file state, and\n- the issue is not already fixed in the worktree\n\n`Already fixed / stale`\n- thread is outdated, or\n- the code already addresses it, or\n- the comment refers to an earlier diff shape that no longer exists\n\n`Non-code noise`\n- Graphite merge queue comments\n- usage-limit notices\n- bot bookkeeping without code findings\n\n### 7. Verify against the current worktree\n\nBefore editing:\n\n1. open the current file in the worktree\n2. inspect the exact code around the commented path/line\n3. decide whether the review comment is still valid now\n\nDo not trust the review diff alone. Many bot comments are attached to old hunks and become stale after subsequent pushes.\n\n### 8. Fix only the valid items\n\nWhen fixing:\n\n- prefer narrow fixes tied to the still-valid review findings\n- add focused regression coverage when practical\n- keep unrelated local or `.conduct/` artifacts out of commits unless the user explicitly asks for them\n\n### 9. Report with explicit buckets\n\nSummarize findings as:\n\n- `Fixed`\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed)`\n- `Not Attempted`\n\nIf something remains pending because of extracted-worktree dependency issues, say so explicitly.\n\n## Decision Heuristics\n\nUse unresolved threads as the main queue when the user says:\n\n- \"check newer PR comments\"\n- \"there are still comments\"\n- \"you missed some issues\"\n- \"refetch review feedback\"\n\nUse timestamp filtering only to answer narrower questions like:\n\n- \"what comments arrived after commit <sha>?\"\n- \"what was posted after the last push?\"\n\nEven then, compare the result against current unresolved threads before concluding there is nothing new.\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n- Missing comments because `gh pr view --json comments` does not include review threads.\n- Missing comments because `pulls/<n>/comments` was queried with the wrong cutoff time.\n- Treating all old comments as irrelevant even when the thread is still unresolved.\n- Treating all unresolved bot comments as actionable without checking the current code.\n- Forgetting that some PR activity is non-code noise, especially merge-queue comments.\n\n## Minimum Successful Pass\n\nA correct refetch pass should:\n\n1. fetch unresolved review threads with GraphQL\n2. inspect review bodies and issue comments\n3. cross-check against the current worktree\n4. separate valid issues from stale ones\n5. only then start fixing code\n55:- SKI-6, McpFn, @mcpfn/auth@0.0.2, @mcpfn/core@0.0.2, @mcpfn/testing@0.0.2, pnpm mcpfn:verify, reviewThreads, PR #7, f9bc1d86, 4754f954\n76:- Start PR triage with unresolved GraphQL `reviewThreads`, then use review bodies, issue comments, and inline comments as supplemental evidence. Validate every finding in the exact-head worktree and stop after the bounded authorized round. [Task 1]\n96:- splane pr-review-fix, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, PR #567, PR #568, 69723719, 9c576dc, 41e1efbb, reviewThreads, legacy-product-keys, svelte-check\n358:# Task Group: August 29 Nucleus TIDY-442 review planning and immutable Diffpanel publication\n362:## Task 1: Assess the entire TIDY-442 changeset for easy reviews, partial\n370:- TIDY-442, stacked PRs, DataFn, Flux, origin/dev, datafn-migration-inventory.md, file: dependencies, extension/clipper, Playwright, git add -p\n489:- MCP-2, McpFn, work-linear-issue@1.0.1, oauth-core 0.0.2, tsup, ESM, CommonJS, gate:mcpfn-release, reviewThreads, force-with-lease, PR-122, UNSTABLE\n903:- splane pr-review-fix, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, PR #3, SKI-1, d398cba6, reviewThreads, outdated, repeatable-read, applicationCommit\n934:- `21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1` uses unresolved GraphQL threads plus review bodies, issue comments, and inline comments as one snapshot; after an authorized push, verify heads once and do not refetch/poll. An unresolved thread can be historical: inspect `outdated`, current code/commit, and addressed markers. [Task 1]\n995:- PR #116, SFNS-2, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, reviewThreads, nodeType, undefined versus null, authfn/docs/static/llms-full.txt, MCPFn, UIFn, d27cceb2\n1075:- DEV-1, splane, 21n/work-linear-issue@1.0.1, next, DEV-1-origin-dev, devfn, Turbo, Node 22, PR #117, ff824c61, reviewThreads, bounded remediation, merged into dev\n2124:- PR #103, SF-8, `21n/report-and-fix-pr@1.0.0`, `reviewThreads`, GraphQL, Outlook SSRF, checkpoint null, Python rate limit, `0cea01c4`, `git push origin HEAD:SF-8`\n2138:- For private Superfunctions PRs, inspect authenticated current `reviewThreads`/head comments, including `isResolved` and `isOutdated`, before editing. PR #103 used a clean isolated exact-head worktree because the existing checkout was dirty; after user authorization, fetch `origin/SF-8`, require it to equal `HEAD^`, then push `HEAD:SF-8` and verify local/remote/live PR SHA equality. [Task 4]\n2144:- A malformed GraphQL query can fail; use paginated `reviewThreads(first:100, after:$cursor)` and check `pageInfo.hasNextPage`. If `python` is absent from PATH, use the bundled runtime via a temporary `python` symlink before running `node scripts/ci-run-python-package.mjs plugfn/python`; keep full-repository baseline failures separate from changed-surface tests. [Task 4]\n2241:- splane, 21n/report-and-fix-pr, PR #93, BillFn, GraphQL reviewThreads, package-lock.json, Kysely, memory adapter, `gh pr merge 93 --merge`\n2251:- PR #112, ExtFn, reviewThreads, unresolved threads, e7b4853f, 73 tests, billing lock\n2261:- PR #113, ApiFn, reviewThreads, TS5055, .svelte.d.ts, gh pr ready, faea2d04, unresolvedCount:0\n2271:- PR #103, 21n/report-and-fix-pr@1.0.0, SF-8-ci-fixes, reviewThreads, 46 unique findings, Ruff, mypy, PlugFn, 236 review threads, delivery claim token\n2297:- when the user invokes `pr-refetch-fix`, check unresolved GraphQL review threads first, then review bodies, issue comments, and inline comments; do not filter by timestamps only. [Task 3]\n2304:- Related skill: skills/thread-aware-pr-review-triage/SKILL.md. Collect PR metadata, pagination-complete inline comments, submitted reviews, conversation comments, and GraphQL `reviewThreads` before edits; collapse threads into current valid findings and mark stale ones separately. [Task 1][Task 3][Task 6]\n2311:- PR #44 triage should fetch GraphQL `reviewThreads(first:100)` plus submitted reviews, issue comments, and inline comments, then separate current from outdated findings. The validated fixes were canonical `installationId`/deprecated `organizationId` handling, URI-encoded route-placeholder materialization in `AdminClient`, and direct-service rejection of undeclared WatchFn filters. [Task 6]\n3724:- SKI-6, McpFn, @mcpfn/core@0.0.2, pnpm mcpfn:verify, reviewThreads, PR #7, f9bc1d86, 4754f954\n4349:- kgqa, PR 88, service_request, raw_service_response, is_raw_service_response, service_raw_answer, reviewThreads, gh api graphql, uv run pytest\n4378:- For these private repos, live `reviewThreads` from authenticated `gh` / GraphQL were the source of truth; connector fetches returned `404`, so do not rely on unauthenticated PR metadata alone. [Task 1][Task 2]\n4387:- Symptom: private-repo PR fetches say `404` or repository not found. Cause: connector access is insufficient for these repos. Fix: switch quickly to authenticated `gh pr view` and GraphQL `reviewThreads` instead of burning time on connector retries. [Task 1][Task 2]\n4529:scope: Use for July 2026 `btnextjs` review work in `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/repos/btnextjs`, especially when the user wants live `reviewThreads` fixed locally or wants a read-only regression review of current checkout changes. Boundaries: live PR thread state, local diff shape, and baseline lint/tsc noise are checkout-specific and should be revalidated live.\n4539:- btnextjs, PR 682, reviewThreads, gh api graphql, 404, local-only, unstaged, Node 22, eslint, vitest, git diff --check, prettier drift, reshape\n4587:- btnextjs, PR 678, customer-requirements, Title cannot be empty, recursive setTimeout, updateRequirement, AddRequirementDialog, KGQA write-back, reviewThreads, local-only\n4610:- For private `btnextjs` PR work, live `reviewThreads` from authenticated `gh` / GraphQL remain the reliable source of truth; flat PR metadata is not enough when outdated or invalid comments matter, and connector fetches can still 404 on private repos. [Task 1][Task 2][Task 3][Task 7]\n4633:- Symptom: the PR-review data source says `404` or misses thread state on a private repo. Cause: connector access is insufficient or stale. Fix: switch quickly to authenticated local `gh` / GraphQL `reviewThreads` rather than retrying flat metadata fetches. [Task 2][Task 3]\n4799:- btnextjs, PR 756, SRO-714, gh api graphql, reviewThreads, isResolved, isOutdated, SES, BT_TEST_MODE, NODE_ENV=test\n4818:- Private connector calls returned 404; use authenticated local `gh` plus GraphQL `reviewThreads` for current `isResolved`/`isOutdated` state. For PR #756, the SES test-mode safety gap was substantive and the `org-flags` comment was cosmetic. [Task 1]\n6105:- PR 592, SRO-527, fetchWithContext, useOrganizationContext, reviewThreads, local-only, Don't commit or push yet, filtered tsc\n6123:- PR 670, SRO-619, widgets, reviewThreads, CASE reorder update, fetchWithContext, res.ok, open-first ordering, NewProgramModal, local-only\n6144:- In `btnextjs`, `gh pr view` is routing context; live GraphQL `reviewThreads(first:100)` or the repo helper that fetches them is the reliable source of truth for unresolved/outdated PR comments. [Task 1][Task 4][Task 10]\n6189:- Symptom: a PR review is judged from summary metadata alone. Cause: `gh pr view` was treated as full review state. Fix: fetch live `reviewThreads` and check unresolved/outdated status against the current head SHA. [Task 1][Task 4][Task 10]\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: pr-refetch-fix\ndescription: Refetch the latest GitHub PR review feedback reliably before applying fixes. Use when a user asks to re-check review comments after a push, says there are newer PR comments that were missed, or when timestamp-only PR comment queries may be incomplete or misleading. This skill is for GitHub PR review triage where unresolved review threads, inline comments, review bodies, and issue comments must be checked together.\n---\n\n# PR Refetch Fix\n\nRefetch PR feedback from the right GitHub surfaces before deciding there are no new issues. Do not rely only on a timestamp cutoff against inline review comments.\n\n## Core Rule\n\nWhen the user asks for \"newer PR comments\", \"latest review issues\", or says comments were missed:\n\n1. Check current unresolved review threads first.\n2. Treat timestamp filtering as secondary, not primary.\n3. Verify every still-open thread against the current worktree before fixing or dismissing it.\n\nThe failure mode this skill prevents is:\n\n- querying only `pulls/{n}/comments` after an incorrect cutoff time\n- missing comments posted in review threads or review bodies\n- missing still-unresolved older threads that remain actionable after newer pushes\n\n## Workflow\n\n### 1. Identify the PRs and auth context\n\nUse the user's existing GitHub auth context if they specified one earlier, such as:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\n```\n\nPreserve that context for every `gh` call in the workflow.\n\n### 1a. Work only in the PR's corresponding worktree and branch\n\nBefore checking comments or making fixes:\n\n1. switch to the PR's dedicated worktree\n2. confirm the checked-out branch is the PR head branch\n3. inspect and edit only in that worktree\n\nDo not:\n\n- inspect comments against the main repo checkout if the PR has a separate worktree\n- validate a review comment against `next`, `dev`, or another sibling PR branch\n- make fixes in one worktree and assume they apply to another PR branch\n\nIf multiple PRs are involved, handle each PR in its own worktree independently.\n\nMinimum check:\n\n```bash\ngit branch --show-current\ngit status --short\ngit remote -v\n```\n\nUse the worktree that already backs the PR whenever available. Treat that worktree as the source of truth for:\n\n- whether a comment is already fixed\n- whether a thread is stale\n- what code should be edited\n\n### 2. Do not start with timestamp-only filtering\n\nDo not begin with:\n\n- only `gh pr view --json comments`\n- only `gh api repos/.../pulls/<n>/comments`\n- only \"comments after last pushed commit time\"\n\nThose can miss actionable review feedback.\n\n### 3. Fetch unresolved review threads with GraphQL\n\nThis is the primary source of truth for \"what still needs attention\".\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql -f query='\nquery {\n  repository(owner:\"OWNER\", name:\"REPO\") {\n    pullRequest(number:PR_NUMBER) {\n      reviewThreads(first:100) {\n        nodes {\n          isResolved\n          path\n          comments(first:20) {\n            nodes {\n              body\n              author { login }\n              createdAt\n              outdated\n              url\n              diffHunk\n              originalLine\n              line\n            }\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}'\n```\n\nIf there may be more than 100 threads, page through the connection instead of assuming the first page is complete.\n\n### 4. Also check the other review surfaces\n\nAfter review threads, inspect:\n\n1. PR reviews\n2. PR issue comments\n3. Inline review comments if needed for raw export or extra metadata\n\nUseful commands:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\n```\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\n```\n\nUse these to supplement threads, not replace them.\n\n### 5. Use timestamps carefully\n\nIf the user explicitly wants \"since the last push\", compute the cutoff from the exact pushed commit, not a guessed time.\n\nRules:\n\n- use the exact commit SHA that was pushed\n- use its absolute timestamp in UTC\n- if the user seems confused about \"today\", \"after that push\", or \"latest\", mention exact dates/times\n\nEven with an accurate cutoff, do not exclude unresolved review threads just because the thread started earlier. A still-unresolved thread can remain actionable after later pushes.\n\n### 6. Classify findings before editing\n\nFor each thread or comment:\n\n`Actionable`\n- thread is unresolved, and\n- the comment still matches the current file state, and\n- the issue is not already fixed in the worktree\n\n`Already fixed / stale`\n- thread is outdated, or\n- the code already addresses it, or\n- the comment refers to an earlier diff shape that no longer exists\n\n`Non-code noise`\n- Graphite merge queue comments\n- usage-limit notices\n- bot bookkeeping without code findings\n\n### 7. Verify against the current worktree\n\nBefore editing:\n\n1. open the current file in the worktree\n2. inspect the exact code around the commented path/line\n3. decide whether the review comment is still valid now\n\nDo not trust the review diff alone. Many bot comments are attached to old hunks and become stale after subsequent pushes.\n\n### 8. Fix only the valid items\n\nWhen fixing:\n\n- prefer narrow fixes tied to the still-valid review findings\n- add focused regression coverage when practical\n- keep unrelated local or `.conduct/` artifacts out of commits unless the user explicitly asks for them\n\n### 9. Report with explicit buckets\n\nSummarize findings as:\n\n- `Fixed`\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed)`\n- `Not Attempted`\n\nIf something remains pending because of extracted-worktree dependency issues, say so explicitly.\n\n## Decision Heuristics\n\nUse unresolved threads as the main queue when the user says:\n\n- \"check newer PR comments\"\n- \"there are still comments\"\n- \"you missed some issues\"\n- \"refetch review feedback\"\n\nUse timestamp filtering only to answer narrower questions like:\n\n- \"what comments arrived after commit <sha>?\"\n- \"what was posted after the last push?\"\n\nEven then, compare the result against current unresolved threads before concluding there is nothing new.\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n- Missing comments because `gh pr view --json comments` does not include review threads.\n- Missing comments because `pulls/<n>/comments` was queried with the wrong cutoff time.\n- Treating all old comments as irrelevant even when the thread is still unresolved.\n- Treating all unresolved bot comments as actionable without checking the current code.\n- Forgetting that some PR activity is non-code noise, especially merge-queue comments.\n\n## Minimum Successful Pass\n\nA correct refetch pass should:\n\n1. fetch unresolved review threads with GraphQL\n2. inspect review bodies and issue comments\n3. cross-check against the current worktree\n4. separate valid issues from stale ones\n5. only then start fixing code\n55:- SKI-6, McpFn, @mcpfn/auth@0.0.2, @mcpfn/core@0.0.2, @mcpfn/testing@0.0.2, pnpm mcpfn:verify, reviewThreads, PR #7, f9bc1d86, 4754f954\n76:- Start PR triage with unresolved GraphQL `reviewThreads`, then use review bodies, issue comments, and inline comments as supplemental evidence. Validate every finding in the exact-head worktree and stop after the bounded authorized round. [Task 1]\n96:- splane pr-review-fix, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, PR #567, PR #568, 69723719, 9c576dc, 41e1efbb, reviewThreads, legacy-product-keys, svelte-check\n358:# Task Group: August 29 Nucleus TIDY-442 review planning and immutable Diffpanel publication\n362:## Task 1: Assess the entire TIDY-442 changeset for easy reviews, partial\n370:- TIDY-442, stacked PRs, DataFn, Flux, origin/dev, datafn-migration-inventory.md, file: dependencies, extension/clipper, Playwright, git add -p\n489:- MCP-2, McpFn, work-linear-issue@1.0.1, oauth-core 0.0.2, tsup, ESM, CommonJS, gate:mcpfn-release, reviewThreads, force-with-lease, PR-122, UNSTABLE\n903:- splane pr-review-fix, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, PR #3, SKI-1, d398cba6, reviewThreads, outdated, repeatable-read, applicationCommit\n934:- `21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1` uses unresolved GraphQL threads plus review bodies, issue comments, and inline comments as one snapshot; after an authorized push, verify heads once and do not refetch/poll. An unresolved thread can be historical: inspect `outdated`, current code/commit, and addressed markers. [Task 1]\n995:- PR #116, SFNS-2, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, reviewThreads, nodeType, undefined versus null, authfn/docs/static/llms-full.txt, MCPFn, UIFn, d27cceb2\n1075:- DEV-1, splane, 21n/work-linear-issue@1.0.1, next, DEV-1-origin-dev, devfn, Turbo, Node 22, PR #117, ff824c61, reviewThreads, bounded remediation, merged into dev\n2124:- PR #103, SF-8, `21n/report-and-fix-pr@1.0.0`, `reviewThreads`, GraphQL, Outlook SSRF, checkpoint null, Python rate limit, `0cea01c4`, `git push origin HEAD:SF-8`\n2138:- For private Superfunctions PRs, inspect authenticated current `reviewThreads`/head comments, including `isResolved` and `isOutdated`, before editing. PR #103 used a clean isolated exact-head worktree because the existing checkout was dirty; after user authorization, fetch `origin/SF-8`, require it to equal `HEAD^`, then push `HEAD:SF-8` and verify local/remote/live PR SHA equality. [Task 4]\n2144:- A malformed GraphQL query can fail; use paginated `reviewThreads(first:100, after:$cursor)` and check `pageInfo.hasNextPage`. If `python` is absent from PATH, use the bundled runtime via a temporary `python` symlink before running `node scripts/ci-run-python-package.mjs plugfn/python`; keep full-repository baseline failures separate from changed-surface tests. [Task 4]\n2241:- splane, 21n/report-and-fix-pr, PR #93, BillFn, GraphQL reviewThreads, package-lock.json, Kysely, memory adapter, `gh pr merge 93 --merge`\n2251:- PR #112, ExtFn, reviewThreads, unresolved threads, e7b4853f, 73 tests, billing lock\n2261:- PR #113, ApiFn, reviewThreads, TS5055, .svelte.d.ts, gh pr ready, faea2d04, unresolvedCount:0\n2271:- PR #103, 21n/report-and-fix-pr@1.0.0, SF-8-ci-fixes, reviewThreads, 46 unique findings, Ruff, mypy, PlugFn, 236 review threads, delivery claim token\n2297:- when the user invokes `pr-refetch-fix`, check unresolved GraphQL review threads first, then review bodies, issue comments, and inline comments; do not filter by timestamps only. [Task 3]\n2304:- Related skill: skills/thread-aware-pr-review-triage/SKILL.md. Collect PR metadata, pagination-complete inline comments, submitted reviews, conversation comments, and GraphQL `reviewThreads` before edits; collapse threads into current valid findings and mark stale ones separately. [Task 1][Task 3][Task 6]\n2311:- PR #44 triage should fetch GraphQL `reviewThreads(first:100)` plus submitted reviews, issue comments, and inline comments, then separate current from outdated findings. The validated fixes were canonical `installationId`/deprecated `organizationId` handling, URI-encoded route-placeholder materialization in `AdminClient`, and direct-service rejection of undeclared WatchFn filters. [Task 6]\n3724:- SKI-6, McpFn, @mcpfn/core@0.0.2, pnpm mcpfn:verify, reviewThreads, PR #7, f9bc1d86, 4754f954\n4349:- kgqa, PR 88, service_request, raw_service_response, is_raw_service_response, service_raw_answer, reviewThreads, gh api graphql, uv run pytest\n4378:- For these private repos, live `reviewThreads` from authenticated `gh` / GraphQL were the source of truth; connector fetches returned `404`, so do not rely on unauthenticated PR metadata alone. [Task 1][Task 2]\n4387:- Symptom: private-repo PR fetches say `404` or repository not found. Cause: connector access is insufficient for these repos. Fix: switch quickly to authenticated `gh pr view` and GraphQL `reviewThreads` instead of burning time on connector retries. [Task 1][Task 2]\n4529:scope: Use for July 2026 `btnextjs` review work in `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/repos/btnextjs`, especially when the user wants live `reviewThreads` fixed locally or wants a read-only regression review of current checkout changes. Boundaries: live PR thread state, local diff shape, and baseline lint/tsc noise are checkout-specific and should be revalidated live.\n4539:- btnextjs, PR 682, reviewThreads, gh api graphql, 404, local-only, unstaged, Node 22, eslint, vitest, git diff --check, prettier drif