nd repository structure for the super-functions project—which hosts `@datafn/client`—there is no explicit public API in the 0.1.0 release specifically named \"mutate,\" \"transact,\" \"atomic,\" or \"batch\" as a standalone, top-level exposed interface [1][3]. The framework focuses on reactive data management where synchronization and mutation handling are largely abstracted by the DataFn engine, which manages data delivery and state consistency across browser and server contexts [1][4]. While other data management libraries (such as those following JSON API standards or other reactive data clients) explicitly provide \"atomic\" or \"batch\" mutation APIs [5][6][7][8], DataFn manages these concerns internally within its own synchronization protocols [1]. Developers using DataFn typically interact with the framework through its reactive data-binding features rather than manually constructing atomic transaction batches [1]. If you are looking for specific transaction control, please consult the official DataFn documentation at datafn.dev, as the library's internal RPC and transport surfaces are continuously updated to support features like extension bridge proxies and improved subscription management [1][3].\n\nCitations:\n\n- 1: https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/blob/dev/README.md\n- 2: https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions\n- 3: https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/commit/c3c37b9428549c2f84c1d2f1e96c2930b9af20da\n- 4: https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/tree/dev/extfn\n- 5: https://dataclient.io/docs/concepts/atomic-mutations\n- 6: https://docs.gluonic.dev/guides/client/mutations\n- 7: https://github.com/GearSite/JSON-API/blob/HEAD/docs/atomic-operations.md\n- 8: https://tanstack.com/db/latest/docs/guides/mutations.md\n\n---\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n\n```shell\n#!/bin/bash\nset -eu\nurl='https://registry.npmjs.org/@datafn/client/-/client-0.1.0.tgz'\nprintf '%s\\n' '--- package contents ---'\ncurl -fsSL \"$url\" | tar -tzf - | head -120\nprintf '%s\\