ull/570#discussion_r3886206027\t<!-- cubic:v=74adb245-1155-4663-8f26-acad72481976 --> <!-- cubic:review-run=7522f186-2360-43cc-b9ce-632214bd3d94 --> <!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":8} --> P2: Removing this `<span id=\"global-sync-status\">` orphans a live consumer: `SyncStatusListener.refreshFromGlobal()` calls `document.getElementById(\"global-sync-status\")?.getAttribute(\"data-syncstatus\")` so it can pick up an in-progress sync on mount, and the span was the only place that element was ever created. After this removal the lookup always returns undefine\n16\tclient/layout/layers/UserBaseLayer.svelte\t176\t2026-08-30T23:51:05Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t9aadca2ea9\ttrue\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3890895070\t**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Bridge legacy Flux mutations into DataFn** When unchanged flows such as `nodeStore.create()` or `taskStore.create()` run, `ResourceStore` writes to the Dexie-backed legacy `flux`, not `datafn`. This call starts that independent runtime after DataFn, but nothing imports subsequent Flux mutations; additionally, `SyncLayer.svelte` now invokes only `pullDatafnNow()`, and repo-wide search finds no remaining `flux.sync()` call. Thus n\n17\tclient/layout/layers/UserBaseLayer.svelte\t329\t2026-08-31T01:52:23Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\tce677ed4a4\ttrue\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3891213265\t**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Confirm native backup delivery before suppressing recovery** In an embedded iOS/macOS recovery flow, `downloadLegacyLocalBackup()` starts the asynchronous `fileStore.downloadFromBlob()` upload/handoff without awaiting or validating it, and this branch immediately saves `download_backup_continue`. If the upload fails or returns no URL, the UI still reports success and permanently suppresses the startup recovery prompt even though\n18\tclient/layout/layers/UserBaseLayer.svelte\t439\t2026-08-31T01:52:23Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3891213269\t**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Restore automatic timezone-change capture** When a Pointron or Nucleum user travels and later focuses the app, `onAppear()` no longer detects or records the browser timezone change; the former `refreshTimeZone()` call was removed, and repo-wide search finds no remaining automatic caller of `userPreferences.setTimeZone()`. Consequently DataFn's temporal history remains stale, so calendar boundaries and historical analytics contin\n19\tclient/layout/layers/UserBaseLayer.svelte\t576\t2026-08-29T09:51:56Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3886201424\t**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Keep Flux initialized until all product stores migrate** In the normal Nucleum, Memotron, and Pointron app flow, this now initializes only DataFn, while numerous unchanged product stores still call the exported legacy `flux` instance—for example, `ResourceStore.create()` invokes `flux.mutation()` and node/history code invokes `flux.selectMany()`. That export remains a null cast until `initFlux()` runs, and repo-wide search shows\n20\tclient/layout/layers/UserLayout.svelte\t30\t2026-08-29T09:53:59Z\tcubic-dev-ai\t0c1c83f24e\ttrue\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3886206079\t<!-- cubic:v=3305c83a-6926-4b7c-a3d0-8b2dda285386 --> <!-- cubic:review-run=7522f186-2360-43cc-b9ce-632214bd3d94 --> <!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":8} --> P2: This import makes `AccessMode.POP` passed to `ResourceResolver` a different enum type from its `accessMode` prop, causing the app type check to fail. Keep the legacy import until `ResourceResolver` migrates too, or migrate both together. <details> <summary>Prompt for AI agents</summary> ```text Check if this issue is valid — if so, understand the root cause and \n21\tclient/layout/layers/cache/InMemoryCache.svelte\t37\t2026-08-31T00:39:54Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3891015251\t**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Keep the collection cache mounted in host apps** In normal Nucleum, Memotron, and Pointron flows, this replacement mounts only the global calendar cache and creates signals for total resource counts and node subtypes. The previous `CollectionCache` action was removed, and repo-wide search shows it is now mounted only by the clipper, leaving no host-app producer for `collection-item-counts` or `typed-collection-cache`; consequent\n22\tclient/layout/layers/cache/cache.store.ts\t18\t2026-08-31T00:39:54Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3891015247\t**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Keep dispatching cache update events** When DataFn resource-count or node-subtype signals emit after a consumer has mounted, `cache.replace()` now updates only the internal store. Consumers such as `ResourceSwitcherItem` and `LibrarySubTypeSwitcher` read via `cache.retrieve()` rather than subscribing to the store, and rely exclusively on `ComponentBaseLayer`'s `cacheUpdate` event to refresh, so their displayed counts remain at t\n23\tclient/persistence/legacyLocalDataBackup.ts\t164\t2026-08-31T00:20:18Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3890963089\t**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Convert legacy timezone rows during recovery** For migrated Pointron or Nucleum users with records in the legacy `tz` store, this map has no timezone target, so `resolveLegacyStoreResource(\"tz\")` returns undefined and recovery silently skips the history even though detection recognizes `tz` as a legacy resource store. The replacement timezone store reads only `datafn.temporal.listTimezoneChanges()`, leaving historical timezone c\n24\tclient/persistence/legacyLocalDataBackup.ts\t173\t2026-08-30T23:20:02Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3890827046\t**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Translate PointLog fields before importing** For old Pointron databases containing `PointLog`, mapping the store directly to `sessionLog` is insufficient: historical rows use fields such as `totalFocus`, `totalBreak`, `start`, and `goalId`, while the DataFn schema requires modern fields including `startUnix`, `endUnix`, and `sessionId`. The current transform only renames `goalId`, so filtering against the schema drops the focus \n25\tclient/persistence/legacyLocalDataBackup.ts\t396\t2026-08-29T10:00:24Z\tpullfrog\t0c1c83f24e\ttrue\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3886218145\tLegacy Dexie databases are named `${userOrDapId}-1`, but this accepts every `*-1` database while [`detectLegacyLocalData()`](https://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/blob/e28a5819c6abd13f1ce97f4ab2e8a277ff22b9d1/client/persistence/legacyLocalDataBackup.ts#L201-L235) enumerates the whole browser. On a shared device, account B can therefore import account A's records into B's DataFn namespace; discovery and import need to be scoped to the active identity. <details><summary>Technical details</summary> ````markdown # Scope leg\n26\tclient/persistence/legacyLocalDataBackup.ts\t455\t2026-08-30T18:42:30Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3890210785\t**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Import legacy KV records instead of silently skipping them** When a legacy database contains the `kv` store, `resolveLegacyStoreResource(\"kv\")` returns undefined because `legacyResourceMap` has no KV mapping, so this branch silently discards records such as `kv:globalPreferences`, `kv:preferences`, `kv:uiState`, and `kv:appMenu`. The recovery flow nevertheless persists an `import_old_data` decision and reports success, leaving m\n27\tclient/persistence/legacyLocalDataBackup.ts\t461\t2026-08-30T23:51:05Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3890895071\t**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Normalize renamed resource IDs during legacy import** For imports from renamed stores such as `goal`, `PointGoal`, `todo`, `PointTask`, or `combination`, the target table is changed to `objective`, `task`, or `space`, but the record ID is retained verbatim here; the added tests even preserve `goal:*` IDs in objective rows. DataFn's `determineResourceType()` returns the ID prefix literally, so selecting a recovered goal for `rela\n28\tclient/persistence/legacyLocalDataBackup.ts\t623\t2026-08-30T22:59:12Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3890782300\t**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Add a fallback when database enumeration is unavailable** On browsers or embedded WebViews that implement IndexedDB but not `indexedDB.databases()`, this returns `undefined`, causing `detectLegacyLocalData()` to report `isSupported: false`; `UserBaseLayer` then skips the recovery gate and starts with an empty DataFn database even though the active user's legacy `${userId}-1` or `${dapId}-1` database is still present. Probe the d\n29\tclient/persistence/legacyLocalDataBackup.ts\t1044\t2026-08-30T18:42:30Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3890210782\t**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Rehydrate binary values during legacy import** For legacy `file` records containing `Uint8Array`, `ArrayBuffer`, Blob, or File data, export replaces the bytes with a `__legacyIndexedDbType`/`dataBase64` marker, but this fallback imports that marker as a plain object instead of decoding it. The unchanged file consumers expect `Uint8Array` data, so recovered offline attachments and thumbnails become empty or unreadable even though\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"15\tclient/layout/layers/BaseLayer.svelte\t609\t2026-08-29T09:53:58Z\tcubic-dev-ai\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3886206027\t<!-- cubic:v=74adb245-1155-4663-8f26-acad72481976 --> <!-- cubic:review-run=7522f186-2360-43cc-b9ce-632214bd3d94 --> <!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":8} --> P2: Removing this `<span id=\"global-sync-status\">` orphans a live consumer: `SyncStatusListener.refreshFromGlobal()` calls `document.getElementById(\"global-sync-status\")?.getAttribute(\"data-syncstatus\")` so it can pick up an in-progress sync on mount, and the span was the only place that element was ever created. After this removal the lookup always returns undefine\n16\tclient/layout/layers/UserBaseLayer.svelte\t176\t2026-08-30T23:51:05Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t9aadca2ea9\ttrue\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3890895070\t**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Bridge legacy Flux mutations into DataFn** When unchanged flows such as `nodeStore.create()` or `taskStore.create()` run, `ResourceStore` writes to the Dexie-backed legacy `flux`, not `datafn`. This call starts that independent runtime after DataFn, but nothing imports subsequent Flux mutations; additionally, `SyncLayer.svelte` now invokes only `pullDatafnNow()`, and repo-wide search finds no remaining `flux.sync()` call. Thus n\n17\tclient/layout/layers/UserBaseLayer.svelte\t329\t2026-08-31T01:52:23Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\tce677ed4a4\ttrue\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3891213265\t**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Confirm native backup delivery before suppressing recovery** In an embedded iOS/macOS recovery flow, `downloadLegacyLocalBackup()` starts the asynchronous `fileStore.downloadFromBlob()` upload/handoff without awaiting or validating it, and this branch immediately saves `download_backup_continue`. If the upload fails or returns no URL, the UI still reports success and permanently suppresses the startup recovery prompt even though\n18\tclient/layout/layers/UserBaseLayer.svelte\t439\t2026-08-31T01:52:23Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3891213269\t**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Restore automatic timezone-change capture** When a Pointron or Nucleum user travels and later focuses the app, `onAppear()` no longer detects or records the browser timezone change; the former `refreshTimeZone()` call was removed, and repo-wide search finds no remaining automatic caller of `userPreferences.setTimeZone()`. Consequently DataFn's temporal history remains stale, so calendar boundaries and historical analytics contin\n19\tclient/layout/layers/UserBaseLayer.svelte\t576\t2026-08-29T09:51:56Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3886201424\t**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Keep Flux initialized until all product stores migrate** In the normal Nucleum, Memotron, and Pointron app flow, this now initializes only DataFn, while numerous unchanged product stores still call the exported legacy `flux` instance—for example, `ResourceStore.create()` invokes `flux.mutation()` and node/history code invokes `flux.selectMany()`. That export remains a null cast until `initFlux()` runs, and repo-wide search shows\n20\tclient/layout/layers/UserLayout.svelte\t30\t2026-08-29T09:53:59Z\tcubic-dev-ai\t0c1c83f24e\ttrue\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3886206079\t<!-- cubic:v=3305c83a-6926-4b7c-a3d0-8b2dda285386 --> <!-- cubic:review-run=7522f186-2360-43cc-b9ce-632214bd3d94 --> <!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":8} --> P2: This import makes `AccessMode.POP` passed to `ResourceResolver` a different enum type from its `accessMode` prop, causing the app type check to fail. Keep the legacy import until `ResourceResolver` migrates too, or migrate both together. <details> <summary>Prompt for AI agents</summary> ```text Check if this issue is valid — if so, understand the root cause and \n21\tclient/layout/layers/cache/InMemoryCache.svelte\t37\t2026-08-31T00:39:54Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3891015251\t**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Keep the collection cache mounted in host apps** In normal Nucleum, Memotron, and Pointron flows, this replacement mounts only the global calendar cache and creates signals for total resource counts and node subtypes. The previous `CollectionCache` action was removed, and repo-wide search shows it is now mounted only by the clipper, leaving no host-app producer for `collection-item-counts` or `typed-collection-cache`; consequent\n22\tclient/layout/layers/cache/cache.store.ts\t18\t2026-08-31T00:39:54Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3891015247\t**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Keep dispatching cache update events** When DataFn resource-count or node-subtype signals emit after a consumer has mounted, `cache.replace()` now updates only the internal store. Consumers such as `ResourceSwitcherItem` and `LibrarySubTypeSwitcher` read via `cache.retrieve()` rather than subscribing to the store, and rely exclusively on `ComponentBaseLayer`'s `cacheUpdate` event to refresh, so their displayed counts remain at t\n23\tclient/persistence/legacyLocalDataBackup.ts\t164\t2026-08-31T00:20:18Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3890963089\t**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Convert legacy timezone rows during recovery** For migrated Pointron or Nucleum users with records in the legacy `tz` store, this map has no timezone target, so `resolveLegacyStoreResource(\"tz\")` returns undefined and recovery silently skips the history even though detection recognizes `tz` as a legacy resource store. The replacement timezone store reads only `datafn.temporal.listTimezoneChanges()`, leaving historical timezone c\n24\tclient/persistence/legacyLocalDataBackup.ts\t173\t2026-08-30T23:20:02Z\tchatgpt-codex-connector\t2e46d625da\tfalse\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3890827046\t**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Translate PointLog fields before importing** For old Pointron databases containing `PointLog`, mapping the store directly to `sessionLog` is insufficient: historical rows use fields such as `totalFocus`, `totalBreak`, `start`, and `goalId`, while the DataFn schema requires modern fields including `startUnix`, `endUnix`, and `sessionId`. The current transform only renames `goalId`, so filtering against the schema drops the focus \n25\tclient/persistence/legacyLocalDataBackup.ts\t396\t2026-08-29T10:00:24Z\tpullfrog\t0c1c83f24e\ttrue\thttps://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/pull/570#discussion_r3886218145\tLegacy Dexie databases are named `${userOrDapId}-1`, but this accepts every `*-1` database while [`detectLegacyLocalData()`](https://github.com/21nCo/nucleum/blob/e28a5819c6abd13f1ce97f4ab2e8a277ff22b9d1/client/persistence/legacyLocalDataBackup.ts#L201-L235) enumerates the whole browser. On a shared device, ac